{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792693060,"nonce":"6MuDEEmHpDM4VdEL/CxVpxqnFSk=","message":"Final native verification completed on Apple M1 Pro / Metal. Five new offscreen GPU cases and one CPU hash case pass in SSBO, forced vertex-buffer and forced data-texture modes. BLINC_MULTISTOP_VERTEX=1 was enabled, fallback primitive/glyph capacities were 1024, and all capability overrides are restored (renderer.rs has no diff).\n\nNew coverage includes cubic and quadratic curves, axis-aligned and rotated SVG ellipses checked against independent analytic samples, open/closed contours, same/opposite winding under nonzero/even-odd, push-time DPI/shear/quarter-turn/reflection with the image later emitted under identity, actual tight scratch bakes, background/foreground and static/motion/scratch owners, rectangular scissors, foreground extraction after removing batch aux, three changed/empty-path frames through all application entries, explicit legacy drawing, dynamic overlays, 4x MSAA, public overlay/glass and reduced backdrop routes. Empty, move-only, line-only, non-finite, huge/singular, input-command-budget and actual curve-subdivision-budget paths cover no pixels.\n\nFour new CPU integration tests pass for foreground merging and repeated owner drains, ancestor stripping and rectangular scissors, one-shot rules and topmost selection, and preservation of the non-image native fallback. The unchanged-image CPU hash control varies path contour direction and fill rule while batch aux remains empty.\n\nExisting raw shape-clip, affine, foreground RGBA, foreground shapes, background RGBA, composite and motion app regressions pass in all modes, as do all 33 display-list GPU cases. The remaining 12 normal native GPU regressions (producer opacity, overlay layers, aux routing, fill rule) pass. Existing shape/affine CPU integrations also pass. Logs: /private/tmp/blinc-rgba-path-final-{normal,vb,dt}-*.log.\n\nThe 0.25-pixel tolerance applies to flattening the Bézier curves; SVG arcs use Lyon's cubic approximation before that step. No stricter total error bound for arbitrarily large SVG arcs is claimed. Non-image path coverage is now separately tracked in 732c3370857424d2e3c7ccfcde44fe0095471eda74dae4e5b415daca036738fe. Remaining raw-image corner-shape/overflow-fade properties are separately tracked under the parent in a477b8180872791de7cd560f28ae6f32a66aedb7a95f500f01e4e42f6503aae7. No shared ClipPath/CSS changes, browser/mobile pixels or performance benchmarks.","files":null}]}